# CQ-23  What was the observed time-series value of property P of device D at
#        time T?
# Group F -- SHIFT is EXPECTED TO FAIL this.
#
# Parameters (method note 5): the device and the observation time.
#
# MISSING TERM. SHIFT stores last-capture attributes only, exactly as the CQ
# text says. It declares shift:lastDataCaptureTime, shift:lastReadTime and
# shift:lastResponseTimestamp -- single-valued scalars overwritten on each
# read. There is:
#   - no Observation / Measurement class, so a value cannot be reified
#     together with its time;
#   - no result / hasValue property pairing a magnitude with a timestamp;
#   - no observed-property term, so P cannot be named.
# Consequently the graph holds at most ONE value per device and no history:
# a query for time T is not merely unanswered, it is unrepresentable.
# The nearest equivalents are saref:Measurement (saref:hasTimestamp,
# saref:hasValue, saref:relatesToProperty) and sosa:Observation
# (sosa:resultTime, sosa:hasSimpleResult, sosa:observedProperty).
#
# The query below is the closest SHIFT admits and returns nothing.
PREFIX shift: <https://w3id.org/shift/core#>
PREFIX xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>
SELECT ?device ?lastDataCaptureTime ?lastReadTime
WHERE {
  # -- parameters --------------------------------------------------------
  VALUES ?DEVICE  { <https://w3id.org/shift/kg/asset/METER-000> }
  VALUES ?AT_TIME { "2026-03-05T12:00:00"^^xsd:dateTime }
  # ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  BIND(?DEVICE AS ?device)
  ?DEVICE shift:lastDataCaptureTime ?lastDataCaptureTime .
  OPTIONAL { ?DEVICE shift:lastReadTime ?lastReadTime }
  FILTER(?lastDataCaptureTime = ?AT_TIME)
}
